{"qid":"Q49683","names":["Grand Duchy of Lithuania"],"title":"Grand Duchy of Lithuania","endonym":{"native":"Lietuva","lang":"lt"},"capsule":"The Grand Duchy of Lithuania was a major medieval and early modern state that expanded from its Lithuanian heartland to encompass present-day Belarus, western Ukraine, and parts of Russia and Poland, reaching its greatest extent under Vytautas the Great (r. 1392-1430). Under the Jagiellonian dynasty beginning with the personal union with Poland in 1385, Lithuania became the largest state in Europe by area during the fifteenth century. Internal succession crises and mounting pressure from Moscow gradually reduced its territory; the formal merger with Poland into the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth in 1569 ended the duchy's independence, and the dataset attests it through 1449, reflecting the period before the definitive union. A rump grand duchy persisted nominally until the Third Partition of Poland in 1795.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The duchy's borders are relatively well attested through Jagiellonian-era administrative records, chronicles, and the extensive surviving cartographic tradition; eastern frontier zones with Moscow and Tatars are less precisely delineated and subject to scholarly debate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Grand Duchy of Lithuania","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Grand-Duchy-of-Lithuania"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Grand Duchy of Lithuania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Duchy_of_Lithuania"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Grand Duchy of Lithuania","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Mindaugas"